window.EDUVERSE_BANK_IELTS_PROMPTS = {"writing":[{"id":"IELW101","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the number of visitors per week to a community library in the fictional town of Millfield, by age group, in 2024 and 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Bar chart: weekly library visitors by age group (Under 18, 18-34, 35-54, 55 and over) for 2024 and 2025. The figures below are original practice data created for this exercise. They are not real-world statistics. Data: Under 18: 2024 = 120, 2025 = 165. 18-34: 2024 = 210, 2025 = 190. 35-54: 2024 = 180, 2025 = 205. 55 and over: 2024 = 150, 2025 = 230.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW102","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The line graph below shows average monthly rainfall, in millimetres, in the fictional coastal town of Seabrook over one year. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Line graph: average monthly rainfall in millimetres, January to December, for Seabrook. The figures below are original practice data created for this exercise. They are not real-world statistics. Data (mm): Jan 95, Feb 80, Mar 70, Apr 55, May 45, Jun 30, Jul 25, Aug 35, Sep 60, Oct 85, Nov 110, Dec 120.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW103","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The table below shows the population, number of schools, and number of parks in three fictional districts of the city of Riverton in 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Table with three districts (Northside, Lakeside, Old Town) and three columns (population, number of schools, number of parks). The figures below are original practice data created for this exercise. They are not real-world statistics. Data: Northside: population 48,000; schools 12; parks 6. Lakeside: population 32,000; schools 9; parks 11. Old Town: population 21,000; schools 5; parks 3.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW104","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The diagram below shows the parts of a simple solar cooker and how it works. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Diagram of a box-type solar cooker: a dark, insulated box with a transparent glass lid, a hinged reflective panel that directs sunlight into the box, and a black cooking pot placed on a raised shelf inside the box. Arrows show sunlight striking the reflective panel, entering through the glass lid, and being absorbed as heat by the black pot, while the insulated walls reduce heat loss. All labels and descriptions are original material created for this exercise.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW105","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The diagram below shows how a damaged book is repaired in the workshop of a fictional school library. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main stages of the process. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Process diagram with six stages: (1) the librarian inspects the damaged book, notes torn pages, a loose spine, or a damaged cover, and attaches a repair slip; (2) the book is cleaned: dust is brushed off, pencil marks are erased, and bent pages are flattened under a weight; (3) torn pages are mended with clear tape and loose pages are fixed back into place; (4) the spine is repaired: old glue is removed, fresh glue is applied, cloth tape is pressed along the spine, and the book is held in a clamp; (5) a new protective cover is fitted, and the label and barcode are replaced if they are damaged; (6) the repaired book is checked, stamped, and returned to the shelf for borrowing. The figures below are original practice data created for this exercise. They are not real-world statistics. Practice example: in one week the workshop repairs 18 books — 7 with torn pages, 6 with loose spines, 3 with damaged covers, and 2 with more than one problem; the average repair time in the example is 25 minutes per book.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW106","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The pie charts below show how households in a fictional survey divided their monthly spending in 2015 and 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Two pie charts: household monthly spending shares in 2015 and 2025, in five categories (Housing, Food, Transport, Leisure, Other). The figures below are original practice data created for this exercise. They are not real-world statistics. Data 2015: Housing 30%, Food 25%, Transport 15%, Leisure 12%, Other 18%. Data 2025: Housing 38%, Food 22%, Transport 12%, Leisure 15%, Other 13%.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW107","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the percentage of people in four age groups who took part in three types of sport in a fictional survey. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Grouped bar chart: percentage of people taking part in swimming, running, and cycling, for age groups 16-24, 25-39, 40-59, and 60 and over. The figures below are original practice data created for this exercise. They are not real-world statistics. Data: 16-24: swimming 42%, running 55%, cycling 38%. 25-39: swimming 35%, running 48%, cycling 44%. 40-59: swimming 30%, running 32%, cycling 35%. 60 and over: swimming 28%, running 12%, cycling 20%.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW108","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The table below shows enrolment in four evening courses at a fictional adult learning centre in three terms of one year. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Table: enrolment numbers for four evening courses (English, Computing, Cooking, Photography) in Term 1, Term 2, and Term 3. The figures below are original practice data created for this exercise. They are not real-world statistics. Data: English: 85, 92, 110. Computing: 70, 66, 58. Cooking: 45, 60, 63. Photography: 30, 38, 35.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW109","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The diagrams below show the ground floor of a fictional public library before and after a rearrangement. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Two floor-plan diagrams, 'Before' and 'After'. Before: the entrance is in the middle of the south wall; the information desk stands directly north of the entrance, in the south-centre; a reading room occupies the whole west side, from the north-west corner to the south-west corner; book shelves fill the centre, east of the reading room; a small children's corner sits in the north-east corner, east of the book shelves; a storage room occupies the south-east corner, south of the children's corner and east of the book shelves. After: the entrance and the information desk are unchanged, in the south-centre. A new computer room occupies the north-west corner, taking the northern third of the former reading room. The reading room is reduced to the southern two-thirds of the west side. The children's area is enlarged in place: it now occupies the whole north-east area, covering the old north-east corner plus the north-centre strip formerly used by the northern book shelves; it does not extend to the north-west, which is the computer room. The book shelves remain in the centre but are narrower, because their northern part has given way to the enlarged children's area. A new quiet study area occupies the south-east corner, replacing the storage room; the storage room is removed from the ground floor, and its contents are moved to a cupboard behind the information desk. The plans are original material created for this exercise.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW110","tugas":"Task 1","prompt":"The diagram below shows how compost is made from food scraps in a fictional school garden project. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main stages of the process. Write at least 150 words.","visual":"Process diagram with six stages: (1) food scraps are collected from the school canteen bins and separated from plastic, metal, and other unwanted material; (2) the scraps are chopped into small pieces and mixed in layers with dry leaves and shredded cardboard inside a compost bin; (3) the mixture is watered lightly and covered with a lid to keep it damp and warm; (4) once a week the mixture is turned over with a garden fork to let air in, and its dampness is checked; (5) after several weeks the dark, crumbly material is passed through a sieve, and any large pieces that remain are put back into the bin for further breaking down; (6) the finished compost is packed into bags and spread on the school garden beds. The figures below are original practice data created for this exercise. They are not real-world statistics. Practice example: 40 kilograms of food scraps collected in one week, mixed with 10 kilograms of dry leaves and cardboard, produce about 12 kilograms of finished compost after eight weeks.","minKata":150,"waktuMenit":20,"kriteria":["Task achievement","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW201","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"Some people believe that cities should give priority to bicycles over cars by building more cycle lanes and restricting car use in city centres. Others believe that cars remain essential and should not be restricted. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW202","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"Some people argue that primary school children should not be given homework, because young children need time to play and rest. Others argue that homework helps children develop good study habits. To what extent do you agree or disagree with giving homework to primary school children? Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW203","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"Working from home has become common for many office workers. What are the advantages and disadvantages of working from home, for workers and for employers? Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW204","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"Some people think that museums should be free for everyone, so that all people can learn about history and culture. Others think that museums should charge visitors in order to pay for their running costs. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW205","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"In many countries, young people are leaving the countryside to live and work in cities, while older people remain in rural areas. What problems does this cause, and what solutions can you suggest? Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW206","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"Advertising aimed at children should be strictly restricted, because children cannot judge advertising messages in the same way as adults. To what extent do you agree or disagree? Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW207","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"Some experts believe that children should begin learning a foreign language in primary school, while others believe that secondary school is early enough. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW208","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"With the rise of the internet and digital books, some people argue that public libraries are no longer necessary. Others argue that libraries still play an important role in communities. To what extent do you agree or disagree that public libraries are still needed? Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW209","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"Tourism brings income to local communities, but it can also damage the environment and change local ways of life. Do the advantages of tourism outweigh the disadvantages for local communities? Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]},{"id":"IELW210","tugas":"Task 2","prompt":"University education should be free for all students, with the cost paid by the government through taxation. To what extent do you agree or disagree? Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience. Write at least 250 words.","minKata":250,"waktuMenit":40,"kriteria":["Task response","Coherence and cohesion","Lexical resource","Grammatical range and accuracy"]}],"speaking":[{"id":"IELS01","part1":["Where is your hometown?","What do you like most about living there?","Has your hometown changed much in recent years?","Would you like to live in your hometown in the future? Why, or why not?","What is the most interesting place to visit in your hometown?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe a place in your hometown that you enjoy visiting.","poin":["where this place is","what you can do or see there","how often you go there","and explain why you enjoy visiting this place."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["Why do some people prefer living in a small town rather than a big city?","How can towns keep their local character while they grow?","Do you think young people today feel less attached to their hometowns than older generations did? Why?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."},{"id":"IELS02","part1":["Do you like cooking? Why, or why not?","What kinds of food do you usually eat at home?","Who usually cooks in your family?","Have you ever tried cooking a dish from another country?","Do you prefer eating at home or eating out?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe a meal that you enjoyed sharing with other people.","poin":["when and where the meal took place","who you shared the meal with","what food was served","and explain why you enjoyed this meal."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["Why do people enjoy eating together, in your opinion?","How have eating habits changed in your country in recent years?","Do you think cooking skills should be taught at school? Why, or why not?","What effect does fast food have on traditional cooking?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."},{"id":"IELS03","part1":["Are you studying or working at the moment?","What subject are you studying, or what is your job?","Why did you choose this subject or job?","What do you find most difficult about your studies or work?","What are your plans for the future?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe a skill that you would like to learn in the future.","poin":["what the skill is","why you would like to learn it","how you would learn it","and explain how this skill would be useful to you."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["Why is it important for adults to keep learning new skills?","How has the internet changed the way people learn new skills?","Do you think practical skills are more valuable than academic knowledge? Why, or why not?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."},{"id":"IELS04","part1":["Do you like travelling? Why, or why not?","What is the most interesting place you have visited?","Do you prefer travelling alone or with other people?","How do you usually travel on long journeys?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe a journey that you remember well.","poin":["where you went","how you travelled there","who you travelled with","and explain why you remember this journey well."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["What are the benefits of travelling to other countries?","How has travel changed in the last twenty years?","Do you think tourism does more good than harm for the places that tourists visit? Why?","Should governments encourage people to travel within their own country? Why, or why not?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."},{"id":"IELS05","part1":["How much time do you spend using your phone each day?","What do you mainly use your phone for?","Do you think people spend too much time on their phones?","Has your phone use changed over the past few years?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe a piece of technology that you find useful in your daily life.","poin":["what the technology is","when you started using it","what you use it for","and explain why you find it useful."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["How has technology changed the way people communicate?","What problems can arise when people depend too much on technology?","Do you think older people find it harder to adapt to new technology? Why, or why not?","How might technology change daily life in the next twenty years?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."},{"id":"IELS06","part1":["Do you exercise regularly? Why, or why not?","What kind of exercise do you enjoy most?","Did you play any sports when you were a child?","Do you think schools should spend more time on physical education?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe a sport or physical activity that you enjoy watching or taking part in.","poin":["what the sport or activity is","when and where you watch it or take part in it","who you do it with, if anyone","and explain why you enjoy it."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["Why do some people find it hard to exercise regularly?","What can governments do to encourage people to be more active?","Do you think watching sport on television encourages people to exercise themselves? Why, or why not?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."},{"id":"IELS07","part1":["What kinds of music do you like listening to?","When do you usually listen to music?","Can you play a musical instrument? Why, or why not?","Has your taste in music changed over time?","Do you prefer listening to music alone or with other people?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe a song or piece of music that is important to you.","poin":["what the song or piece of music is","when you first heard it","what it reminds you of","and explain why it is important to you."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["Why does music play an important role in most cultures?","How has the way people listen to music changed in recent years?","Should music be a compulsory subject at school? Why, or why not?","Do you think live performances are better than recorded music? Why?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."},{"id":"IELS08","part1":["Do you worry about the environment? Why, or why not?","What do you do in your daily life to protect the environment?","Is recycling common where you live?","What environmental problems affect your local area?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe an environmental problem in your country that concerns you.","poin":["what the problem is","what causes it","what effects it has","and explain what you think could be done about it."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["Whose responsibility is it to protect the environment: individuals, companies, or governments?","Can small actions by individuals make a real difference to environmental problems? Why, or why not?","How can schools teach children to care for the environment?","Do you think environmental problems will improve or worsen in the future? Why?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."},{"id":"IELS09","part1":["Do you have many friends? Why, or why not?","What qualities do you value most in a friend?","How did you meet your closest friend?","Do you stay in touch with friends from your childhood?","Is it easy to make new friends as an adult?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe a friend who has had a positive influence on you.","poin":["who this friend is","how you met this friend","what this friend did that influenced you","and explain why this influence was positive."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["How have friendships changed with the rise of social media?","Do you think it is possible to have a close friendship with someone you have only met online? Why, or why not?","Why do friendships sometimes end?","Is friendship as important as family? Why, or why not?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."},{"id":"IELS10","part1":["Do you like reading? Why, or why not?","What kinds of books do you enjoy most?","Do you prefer printed books or e-books?","When did you last read a book for pleasure?","Did you read much as a child?"],"part2":{"topik":"Describe a book that you enjoyed reading.","poin":["what the book is about","when and why you read it","who recommended it to you, if anyone","and explain why you enjoyed reading it."],"instruksi":"You should talk for 2 minutes. You have 1 minute to prepare."},"part3":["Why do some people read less today than in the past?","How can parents encourage children to enjoy reading?","Do you think reading fiction is as valuable as reading non-fiction? Why, or why not?","Will printed books disappear in the future? Why, or why not?"],"catatan":"Part 1: 4-5 minutes. Part 2: 1 minute preparation, 2 minutes speaking. Part 3: 4-5 minutes."}]};
